Registering the subscriber class alone is not enough; it must be registered in the dependency injection container and tagged with `kernel.event_subscriber` [2]. Load it via a `services.php` file placed at `<plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php` [1]. Register the subscriber like this:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/config/services.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

use Swag\BasicExample\Subscriber\MySubscriber;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(MySubscriber::class)
        ->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
};
```

[3] The subscriber must be tagged with `kernel.event_subscriber` to be recognized, and once registered it is loaded at runtime and listens to the mentioned events [3]. If the class is missing from `services.php`, Shopware will not call it, and there may be no obvious error [2].

Create a command class in your plugin that extends Symfony's `Command` and uses the `AsCommand` attribute, for example in `<plugin root>/src/Command/ExampleCommand.php`:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Command/ExampleCommand.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Command;

use Symfony\Component\Console\Attribute\AsCommand;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Command\Command;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Input\InputInterface;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Output\OutputInterface;

#[AsCommand(name: 'swag:example', description: 'Example command')]
class ExampleCommand extends Command
{
    protected function execute(InputInterface $input, OutputInterface $output): int
    {
        $output->writeln('Hello from ExampleCommand');

        return Command::SUCCESS;
    }
}
```

[1]

Then register the command as a service in your plugin's `src/Resources/config/services.php` and tag it with `console.command`:

```php
$services->set(Swag\BasicExample\Command\ExampleCommand::class)
    ->tag('console.command');
```

[1]

Commands registered this way are automatically available via `bin/console` [1]. You can then run it with `bin/console swag:example` [1]. The excerpts do not explicitly state that run command, but it follows from the `name` set in `AsCommand`. [1]

Use service decoration: create a decorator that extends the service's abstract class (Shopware services designed for decoration expose an abstract class as their contract) and wrap the core service via `getDecorated()`. [2] Always delegate to the inner service unless you deliberately replace its implementation, for example:

```php
public function doSomething(): string
{
    // Delegate first, then add your own behavior
    return $this->decoratedService->doSomething(). ' Did something additionally.';
}
```

[2] Type hint the abstract class rather than the concrete implementation in constructors. [2] Where the core offers an extension point, prefer it over decoration, since extension points are subscribed to as events (`.pre`, `.post`, `.error`). [3] Decoration is the approach when there is no extension point and you need to change how an existing service behaves. [1][3] The excerpts do not show the service registration configuration needed to wire a decorator.

When the merchant chooses to keep data, `keepUserData()` returns `true`, and your plugin must not delete persistent data it created. Respect this flag to avoid unintended data loss. [1] The uninstall method receives an `UninstallContext`, which provides the same information as `InstallContext`, plus the `keepUserData()` flag. [3]

```php
// <plugin root>/src/SwagBasicExample.php
public function uninstall(UninstallContext $context): void
{

    if ($uninstallContext->keepUserData()) {
        return;
    }

    // Remove or deactivate the data created by the plugin
}
```

[1]

The documentation also says that when keeping extension data, data such as snippets, media and tables may remain. [2]

Listen to the pagelet's loaded event and add your data to the pagelet object, then display it in an overridden Twig template. Steps from the documentation:

1. Subscribe to the event of the pagelet you want to extend. For the footer, subscribe to `FooterPageletLoadedEvent` [4]:
```php
public static function getSubscribedEvents(): array
{
    return [
        FooterPageletLoadedEvent::class => 'addActiveProductCount'
    ];
}
```
[4]

2. Register the subscriber in the DI container and tag it as an event subscriber [4]:
```php
$services->set(\Swag\BasicExample\Service\AddDataToPage::class)
    ->tag('kernel.event_subscriber');
```
[4]

3. Load the additional data (the documentation recommends a store-api route for this) and add it to the page via the event [4]. The excerpts do not show the code for the handler method body, so that part is not covered here.

4. Display the data in the Storefront by extending the footer template and overriding the block `layout_footer_navigation_columns`. Access the data through `footer.extensions.product_count.count` [3]:
```twig
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig' %}

{% block layout_footer_navigation_columns %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {% if footer.extensions.product_count %}
        <div class="col-md-4 footer-column">
            <p>This shop offers you {{ footer.extensions.product_count.count }} products</p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}
```
[3]

The guide's example template is at `Resources/views/storefront/layout/footer/footer.html.twig` [3].

If you need the data to depend on the current page rather than being loaded with the footer, the documentation describes passing extra `headerParameters` via the `base_esi_header` block and `StorefrontRenderEvent` for the header and footer [2]. The excerpts do not show the equivalent for custom pagelet data in general.

Create a database table first with a migration, then add the DAL entity definition that maps to that table [2]. Requirements include understanding plugin migrations and dependency injection, since the entity definition is registered as a service [1].

Create a definition class that extends `EntityDefinition` and implements `getEntityName` and `defineFields` [5]:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Core/Content/Example/ExampleDefinition.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Core\Content\Example;

use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityDefinition;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\FieldCollection;

class ExampleDefinition extends EntityDefinition
{
    public const ENTITY_NAME = 'swag_example';

    public function getEntityName(): string
    {
        return self::ENTITY_NAME;
    }

    protected function defineFields(): FieldCollection
    {
        return new FieldCollection([]);
    }
}
```

The value returned by `getEntityName` is used as the database table name and as the repository name in the DI container (`<the-name>.repository`), so it must match your table name exactly [5]. The `defineFields` method contains all the fields of your entity [5].

The provided documentation does not show how to register the definition as a service, so that step is not covered in detail here [1].

Create a `Criteria` object and add an `EqualsFilter` on the `name` field, then pass it to the repository's `search` method:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));

    $products = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context);
}
```

This searches for all products with the name `Example name` and returns an `EntitySearchResult` containing the matched products, which you can iterate with a `foreach` [4]. The `EqualsFilter` class is `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Search\Filter\EqualsFilter` [4]. Available fields can be found in the entity's definition, such as `ProductDefinition` for products [4].

Use `getAssociation` on the criteria instead of `addAssociation`, then apply a `RangeFilter` to the association's own criteria. The `RangeFilter` class is `Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\Search\Filter\RangeFilter` [1]. Example:

```php
public function readData(Context $context): void
{
    $criteria = new Criteria();
    $criteria->addFilter(new EqualsFilter('name', 'Example name'));

    $criteria->getAssociation('productReviews')->addFilter(new RangeFilter('points', [
        RangeFilter::GTE => 4
    ]));

    $product = $this->productRepository->search($criteria, $context)->first();
}
```
[2]

This loads the product and adds only the matching `productReviews` to it. The product itself is not excluded if it has no reviews with 4 or more points [1]. If you want to return only products that have at least one such review, filter on `productReviews.points` with `addFilter` instead [1].

Use the `upsert` method on the repository. Provide an `id` in the data, otherwise the data will always be created and never updated [1]. For example, in PHP:

```php
$this->productRepository->upsert([[
    'id' => '<your product ID here>',
    'exampleExtension' => [
        'customString' => 'foo bar'
    ]
]], $context);
```

[3]

In a Twig script service context, use `services.writer.upsert()` with the entity name and payload. If the payload contains an `id` that already exists, it updates the entity; otherwise it creates it [2]:

```twig
{% do services.writer.upsert('product', [
    { 'id':  hook.productId, 'active': true }
]) %}
```

[2]

Create an extension class that extends `EntityExtension` in `<plugin root>/src/Extension/`, and override `extendFields` to add your field. For the product entity, use a subdirectory `Content/Product/` and return `ProductDefinition::ENTITY_NAME` from `getEntityName` [2]. Example:

```php
// <plugin root>/src/Extension/Content/Product/CustomExtension.php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Extension\Content\Product;

use Shopware\Core\Content\Product\ProductDefinition;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\EntityExtension;
use Shopware\Core\Framework\DataAbstractionLayer\FieldCollection;

class CustomExtension extends EntityExtension
{
    public function extendFields(FieldCollection $collection): void
    {
        $collection->add(
            // new fields here
        );
    }

    public function getEntityName(): string
    {
        return ProductDefinition::ENTITY_NAME;
    }
}
```
[2]

Then register the extension via the DI container [2]. To store the data in the database, you cannot add a column to the `product` table; instead, add a new table linked with a OneToOne association, adding a `OneToOneAssociationField` in `extendFields` [3]. Its parameters are `propertyName`, `storageName`, `referenceField`, `referenceClass` and `autoload` [3]. Without a database, you can add a struct value at load time by subscribing to `ProductEvents::PRODUCT_LOADED_EVENT` and calling `addExtension` on each product, registered with the `kernel.event_subscriber` tag [6]. Simple scalar values can alternatively be stored as custom fields, which use a JSON field [4]. The excerpts do not cover the exact details of the database table and definition classes beyond these parameters.

Create your custom template at `<plugin root>/src/Resources/views/storefront/layout/header/logo.html.twig`, using the same directory structure starting from the `views` directory so it is loaded automatically [2]. Add the following at the very beginning of the file to extend the original template [1]:

```twig
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/header/logo.html.twig' %}
```

Then override the block `layout_header_logo_link` by adding the same block with your contents [1]:

```twig
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/layout/header/logo.html.twig' %}

{% block layout_header_logo_link %}
    <h2>Hello world!</h2>
{% endblock %}
```

To append to the logo instead of replacing it, use <code v-pre>{{ parent() }}</code> in your override [1]. Finally, clear the cache with `./bin/console cache:clear` and make sure your plugin is activated and your theme is assigned to the correct sales channel [1].

Pass a CSS selector as the third argument to `PluginManager.register()` in your plugin's `main.js`. The plugin then runs only if the page contains at least one element with that attribute [1]:

```javascript
// <plugin root>/src/Resources/app/storefront/src/main.js
// Import all necessary Storefront plugins
import ExamplePlugin from './example-plugin/example-plugin.plugin';

// Register your custom Storefront plugin
const PluginManager = window.PluginManager;
PluginManager.register('ExamplePlugin', ExamplePlugin, '[data-example-plugin]');
```

Inside the plugin, `this.el` gives access to the DOM element it is bound to [1]. To make the plugin active on pages, add an element with that attribute to your templates, for example `<template data-example-plugin></template>` [2].

To avoid including the plugin in the main bundle, you can use a dynamic import instead, so it loads only when the selector is found on the page [2].

Create a controller that extends `StorefrontController` and sets the route scope on the class, as in `#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StorefrontRouteScope::ID]])]` [1][2]. Add a method with a `Route` attribute, for example `#[Route(path: '/example', name: 'frontend.example.example', methods: ['GET'])]`, which renders a template with `renderStorefront` [1]. Use a route name with the prefix `frontend`, `widgets`, or `payment` (or `api`/`store-api`) so it is identified as a Storefront route [1].

```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Storefront/Controller/ExampleController.php]
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace Swag\BasicExample\Storefront\Controller;

use Shopware\Core\PlatformRequest;
use Shopware\Storefront\Framework\Routing\StorefrontRouteScope;
use Shopware\Storefront\Controller\StorefrontController;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;
use Symfony\Component\Routing\Attribute\Route;

#[Route(defaults: [PlatformRequest::ATTRIBUTE_ROUTE_SCOPE => [StorefrontRouteScope::ID]])]
class ExampleController extends StorefrontController
{
    #[Route(path: '/example', name: 'frontend.example.example', methods: ['GET'])]
    public function showExample(): Response
    {
        return $this->renderStorefront('@SwagBasicExample/storefront/page/example.html.twig', [
            'example' => 'Hello world'
        ]);
    }
}
```

[1]

Register the controller in the DI container and make it public, with the `call` method setting the container [4]:

```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/config/services.php]
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

use Swag\BasicExample\Storefront\Controller\ExampleController;
use Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\ContainerConfigurator;

use function Symfony\Component\DependencyInjection\Loader\Configurator\service;

return static function (ContainerConfigurator $configurator): void {
    $services = $configurator->services();

    $services->set(ExampleController::class)
        ->public()
        ->call('setContainer', [service('service_container')]);
};
```

[4]

Import the controllers so Shopware finds the route attributes [4]:

```php [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/config/routes.php]
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

use Symfony\Component\Routing\Loader\Configurator\RoutingConfigurator;

return static function (RoutingConfigurator $routes): void {
    $routes->import('../../Storefront/Controller/*Controller.php', 'attribute');
};
```

[4]

Finally, create the template at `<plugin root>/src/Resources/views/storefront/page/example.html.twig` [4]:

```twig [PLUGIN_ROOT/src/Resources/views/storefront/page/example.html.twig]
{% sw_extends '@Storefront/storefront/base.html.twig' %}

{% block base_content %}
    <h1>Our example controller!</h1>
{% endblock %}
```

[4]

The excerpts do not show how the template path `@SwagBasicExample/storefront/page/example.html.twig` maps to the file location beyond the example given, so verify that mapping in your plugin.

The documentation describes two approaches. With App Scripts, you can add a discount using the `discount()` function, which requires a price definition created beforehand. Check whether the discount already exists first, since the script may run multiple times:

```twig
// Resources/scripts/cart/my-cart-script.twig
{% set discountPrice = services.cart.price.create({
    'default': { 'gross': 19.99, 'net': 19.99},
    'EUR': { 'gross': 19.99, 'net': 19.99},
}) %}

{% if not services.cart.has('my-custom-discount') %}
    {% do services.cart.discount('my-custom-discount', 'absolute', discountPrice, 'my.custom.discount.label'|trans) %}
{% endif %}
```[2]

A relative discount can be added with a percentage value instead of a price definition [2]:

```twig
// Resources/scripts/cart/my-cart-script.twig
{% do services.cart.discount('my-custom-discount', 'percentage', 10, 'A custom 10% discount') %}
```[2]

In a PHP plugin, create a `CartProcessorInterface` implementation. Inside `process`, set a `PercentagePriceDefinition` on the discount line item, calculate its price with `PercentagePriceCalculator`, and add it to `$toCalculate` [5]. The processor is registered in `services.php` with the tag `shopware.cart.processor` and a priority such as `4500` [3]. Discount line items should be set as non-stackable and non-removable [3][5].

The documentation also notes that changing a product's price directly should rarely be done; adding a discount or surcharge is generally recommended instead [1].
